The fix is complete. Here's what I changed and why.

## The bug

In `getColumnsForNewDataPart` (`src/Storages/MergeTree/MutateTask.cpp`), the wide-part branch that computes a renamed column's type used the wrong condition. When a column in the new schema is the *target* of a rename (some other column was renamed into its name), it must take its type from the **source** column in the rename map — not from the same-named old column.

The old code only entered the correct type-lookup path when `was_removed` was true (the `DROP COLUMN xxx, RENAME yyy TO xxx` pattern). For a **name swap** (`RENAME A TO B, RENAME B TO A`), neither column is removed, so it fell into the `else` branch and assigned `it->type = source_col->second` — the type of the *same-named* source column that was renamed *away*, which is exactly backwards.

## The fix

At `MutateTask.cpp:868`, I changed the condition from `if (was_removed)` to `if (renamed_columns_to_from.contains(it->name))`, so the type-lookup-from-source branch is taken whenever the column is a rename target — covering both the DROP+RENAME pattern *and* name swaps.

I also replaced the hard `LOGICAL_ERROR` throw (when the renamed-from source column isn't present in the part) with erasing the column from the list:

```cpp
if (!maybe_name_and_type)
{
    /// The source column was never materialized ... so it has no
    /// data in the part. Drop it from the column list and let the
    /// reader default-fill it at read time.
    it = storage_columns.erase(it);
    continue;
}
```

This handles the case where one swapped column was added via `ALTER ADD COLUMN` but never filled — the part omits it and the reader default-fills it, instead of raising an exception.

## Coverage of the requirements

- **Wide parts** (`min_bytes_for_wide_part = 0`) flow through this loop, so swaps and DROP+RENAME now record the correct per-column types.
- **Compact parts** return `updated_header.getNamesAndTypesList()` early (line 779); their types come from the `MutationsInterpreter`, which resolves `READ_COLUMN` targets against the new metadata schema, so they were already correct — the test exercises both.
- **Unmaterialized source column**: handled by the `erase` path (both here and in the pre-existing branch at line 828), so no `LOGICAL_ERROR`, and the column is default-filled.
- **Rename combined with a forced rewrite (UPDATE)**: same code path — the rename map lookup and erase logic apply regardless of whether the part is rewritten.

I did not touch anything under `tests/`.
